6074219, -0.041839599609375, -0.002704620361328125, -0.0024471282958984375, -0.004261016845703125, 0.0188140869140625, -0.01325225830078125, -0.007465362548828125, -0.004268646240234375, -0.0008435249328613281, -0.028106689453125, 0.0269622802734375, -0.0009503364562988281, 0.0276641845703125, 0.0020275115966796875, -0.022125244140625, -0.003879547119140625, -0.005336761474609375, -0.01146697998046875, 0.0010175704956054688, 0.027587890625, -0.0007271766662597656, 0.00942230224609375, -0.0082855224609375, 0.036773681640625, -0.0123138427734375, 0.00588226318359375, -0.0080718994140625, 0.01291656494140625, -0.01401519775390625, -0.007076263427734375, 0.019500732421875, -0.043670654296875, 0.0029544830322265625, -0.0055084228515625, -0.015716552734375, -0.00046062469482421875, 0.0238494873046875, 0.001026153564453125, -0.0467529296875, 0.01183319091796875, -0.027923583984375, -0.018218994140625, -0.0031871795654296875, 0.01336669921875, -0.011077880859375, -0.0160980224609375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Componentwise weak convergence of signed synaptic kernels alone does not determine the fast-synapse limit of a sparse threshold-reset network. Within a causal event protocol featuring clamped refractoriness and smooth positive-delay kernels, we construct two families whose excitatory and inhibitory measures both converge weakly to $\u03b4_0$, yet differ in the microscopic order of spike arrivals. In the excitatory-first family, the target neuron fires; in the inhibitory-first family, it does not. This distinction arises precisely when $x+a-b<\u03b8\\le x+a$. Strict margins ensure this response is robust to perturbations in the target state, aggregate E/I pulse masses, and bounded drift. This macroscopic effect persists on a moderately sparse, Dale-compatible random block graph with $q_N\\to\\infty$ and $q_N/N\\to0$, where both systems share the same graph and initial data. Along any deterministic joint scaling $\\varepsilon_N\\downarrow0$, their population-averaged firing counts differ by $1/2+o_{L^1}(1)$. A bounded-degree construction and subsequent probe demonstrate that this discrepancy is macroscopic and can persist through reset. Fixed positive-delay kernels with finitely many classes admit a stable regime: before grazing, typewise-mixing sparse networks converge to a delayed class mean-field system. For directed Erd\u0151s\u2013R\u00e9nyi graphs, the bound $O_P(\u03bb_N^{-1/2}+\\|\u03c0_N-\u03c0\\|_1)$ holds when $\u03bb_N\\to\\infty$ and $\u03bb_N/N\\to0$. This result separates stable averaging at fixed delay from singular collapse; in the latter, componentwise weak convergence loses the signed arrival-order information essential for the threshold-reset response."}}